gorilla-portable-network-hub (@VERSION@) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ONE FEWER THING TO TYPE, BECAUSE IT WAS MEASURED. 0.9.7 named three
    switched-off services and asked for three Set-Service lines. The third,
    WFDSConMgrSvc, the Wi-Fi Direct Services Connection Manager, was in the
    list on reasoning rather than evidence: Windows 11 builds its hotspot on
    Wi-Fi Direct, so its connection manager looks like it has to be involved.
    It is not a declared dependency of either of the others, which is why it
    was listed last and asked for separately, and why 0.9.7 said out loud that
    it was a judgement call.
  * Settled by running it. Both other services set to Manual, WFDSConMgrSvc
    deliberately left Disabled and Stopped: the tethering manager built, gave
    up a name, a passphrase and a band, and the hotspot then reached
    operational state On with the virtual access point appearing as its own
    adapter. It did not merely report itself configurable. It ran, with the
    service that was supposed to be necessary switched off throughout.
  * So it is out. This is not tidying. A message that names three services
    when two are the problem sends somebody to change something that did not
    matter and destroys the evidence of which change did, so the next person
    to look at that laptop cannot tell. That reasoning was already a test in
    this file and it applies to the list itself, not only to the filtering.
  * A test guards it, because the argument for putting it back is more
    persuasive than the argument for leaving it out, and an argument is beaten
    by a measurement. Anybody adding a third service has to delete that test
    and say why.
  * The whole of 0.9.7's advice is now measured rather than reasoned. Before:
    CreateFromConnectionProfile failed with 0x83120001, which is exactly what
    the Settings page is reporting when it shows an empty box. After the two
    lines: configurable, then On. Undone: 0x83120001 again. The fault returns
    when the change is undone, which is what makes it a cause and not a
    coincidence.
  * Also recorded: SharedAccess never RAN in any of the three runs. It has to
    be startable, not started, which is why the advice asks for Set-Service on
    it and Start-Service only on icssvc.
  * bench/hotspot-services-test.ps1 and bench/hotspot-service-isolation.ps1
    are the two scripts, kept in the repository rather than in somebody's temp
    folder. Both capture the before-state rather than assume it, do the work in
    a try and restore in a finally, which is bench/cable/'s shape and exists
    for the same reason.

  NOTHING IN THIS RELEASE CHANGES ANYTHING ON LINUX.

  116 tests.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  @DATE@


gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.9.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * THE LAPTOP IS NOT BROKEN, IT HAS BEEN SWITCHED OFF. Windows Settings
    answers a request for a mobile hotspot with "We can't set up mobile
    hotspot" and an EMPTY properties box: no name, no password, no band.
    Nothing on that screen mentions a service, so there is nothing to search
    for, and the machine reads as faulty when it is only tuned.
  * The cause is two services set to Disabled: icssvc, the Mobile Hotspot
    Service, and SharedAccess, Internet Connection Sharing. Nearly every
    "speed up Windows" list on the internet turns both off, because almost
    nobody shares a network connection and they look like free memory.
    Measured on the machine this was written on: 150 services disabled, these
    two among them. This is the ordinary state of a second-hand laptop, which
    is the only kind the rooms this is built for have.
  * doctor now says so, names each one in plain words AND by its service name,
    and prints the exact lines to type. Not "enable the services": the gap
    between those two is the entire problem, because somebody who knew how to
    enable a service would not have needed telling.
  * The same text is attached to the hotspot error itself, which is the one
    moment we know for certain somebody is about to walk to Settings and
    press a switch that cannot work.
  * READ AS A NUMBER, NOT AS A WORD. The check reads the Start value out of
    reg query, where REG_DWORD is a type name and 4 is a number. sc.exe would
    have been the obvious tool and prints "START_TYPE : 4 DISABLED", and both
    of those words are translated on a Windows installed in any other
    language. Same trap as connected_addresses() and the same answer.
  * A service that cannot be read at all is reported as fine rather than as
    broken. Being unable to answer is not evidence of a fault, and a
    diagnostic that invents problems is worse than one that stays quiet.
  * It also says what to do when the answer is "you are not allowed": a
    managed laptop is a legitimate thing, the command needs Administrator so
    nobody can follow this advice without already having the right to, and the
    cable path needs none of it.
  * docs/HOW-TO.md gets the same thing written for somebody who is not at a
    terminal, and a row in the troubleshooting table.

  NOTHING IN THIS RELEASE CHANGES ANYTHING ON LINUX. Services being switched
  off in this particular way is not a thing that exists there; the check
  returns an empty list and nothing is printed.

  115 tests. Five are new and four of those are about the message rather than
  the mechanism, because on a screen like this the message IS the feature.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  @DATE@


gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.9.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * DOCTOR TOLD EVERY WINDOWS LAPTOP IT HAD NO WIFI ADAPTER. The line read
    "wifi adapter   none found, so this computer cannot make a network".
    Measured on the machine while it was printing that: an Intel Wi-Fi 6
    AX201, up, connected to a network at 173.3 Mbps. Nothing had been found
    because nothing had looked. net::wifi_interface() shells out to nmcli and
    is compiled to a bare None anywhere that is not Linux, so that branch was
    never a failed search: it was a fixed sentence about the machine, printed
    unconditionally, and false on every Windows box since it was written.
  * The conclusion was right and the reason was wrong, which is the worse of
    the two. This program genuinely cannot create a wifi network on Windows,
    because hotspot_up() is nmcli. But a person can create one in Settings in
    about four taps and then everything here works over it, which is exactly
    what hotspot_up says when you try. Two screens disagreeing about that is
    its own bug. doctor now says the same thing and stops reporting a search
    it did not make.
  * It does not try to name the adapter either. netsh wlan show interfaces
    would, and its output is translated on a Windows installed in any other
    language: the same trap that made connected_addresses() test for
    membership rather than parse a label. The advice is identical whether an
    adapter is present or not, so there is nothing here worth a fragile parse.
  * Guarded by a test, because running the program on the machine in question
    is what caught this and nothing in the suite could have.

  NOTHING IN THIS RELEASE CHANGES ANYTHING ON LINUX. The wording it replaces
  only ever appeared where wifi_interface() was compiled out, which is every
  system except Linux. A Linux build of 0.9.6 behaves exactly as 0.9.5 did,
  and 0.9.5 remains a correct thing to be running there.

  Found by doing what the handover asked: running the program on real Windows
  and reading the output, rather than trusting a suite that passes.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  @DATE@

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.9.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * THE PICKER'S HINT LINE RAN OFF THE EDGE OF THE WINDOW. "c clears every
    tick", added in 0.9.4, pushed that line to 77 columns. The window it was
    photographed in is 72, so it ended "esc goes" and the key for leaving the
    screen was not on it. Shortened to "c clears all", 67 columns, next to the
    66 of the hint it replaces.
  * A guard for it, checked at 72 columns as well as 80. The first version of
    that guard used 80 alone, passed, and would have let the same fault
    through, because 77 fits in 80. 72 is not a guess: it is the width of the
    window in the screenshot, counted from the line that was cut. A terminal
    nobody has maximised is the one to design for.
  * A test that asserted the wording rather than the key. It checked for the
    exact string "c clears every tick", so shortening the hint turned the
    suite red for a reason unrelated to what the test guards, which is that a
    tick held in another folder can be removed at all. It now checks for
    "c clears".

  Cut as its own release rather than folded in, because 0.9.4 is tagged and
  its Linux packages are published. Shipping different code under one number
  is the drift the version test in this repository exists to catch, and doing
  it deliberately would be worse than the fault being fixed.

  No change to the program's size: 1,000,784 bytes, the same as 0.9.4. This
  release removes ten characters from one line and adds a test.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  @DATE@

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.9.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * THE OTHER COMPUTER IS NAMED, NOT NUMBERED. "Get files from another
    computer" listed what it found as "169.254.87.1    4 files". Nothing has
    to be typed to choose it, but a teacher cannot tell which machine four
    numbers and three dots are, and in a room with two of them there is no
    way to find out. It now reads "Teacher's laptop    4 files". The name was
    already travelling in the beacon and on the page, so the numbers were
    standing in front of an answer the program already had.
  * gorilla.local can no longer quietly mean "this computer". The name was a
    fixed constant with no check, so two machines both running this both
    answered to it and each resolved it to ITSELF. Following the sending
    screen's own advice took a person to their own machine, which served a
    page and looked exactly like it had worked. It now asks before promising,
    and when the name is taken it says so and points at the list instead. It
    does not rename to gorilla-2.local: somebody who has to know WHICH name
    to type is worse off than somebody told plainly to use the list.
  * A code for a camera on a cable. "Join by camera" had nothing to offer
    without a wifi password and said so, which left phones and tablets, the
    devices that most need a camera, reading an address off a terminal and
    typing it by hand. It now puts the page address in the code.
  * Ticks made in another folder are named, and one key clears them all.
    Ticking a file while an earlier tick was still held somewhere else gave
    "SEND THE 2 TICKED" with one box ticked on screen and no way to find or
    remove the other. Press c to clear every tick.
  * A file count that had been capped now says so. The walk stops at 100,000
    and records that it stopped; the picker threw that away, so a folder of
    150,000 files was announced as "100001 files chosen" and 50,000 of them
    would never have been sent.
  * cable-get says where the files are going. It printed "to fetch into ..",
    because the default is "." and the sentence ended in a full stop, and ".."
    in a path is the folder ABOVE. It now names the folder in full, and on
    Windows without the \\?\ prefix that canonicalize adds.
  * "0.0 MB in 1 pieces" is now "49 B in 1 piece".
  * The port 80 note gives the remedy for the system it is printed on. It said
    "needs administrator rights on Linux ... install the .deb" on Windows,
    where none of that is true and there is no .deb to install.
  * packaging/check-both-builds.sh builds both targets from the one tree and
    checks that per-system text landed only in the system it belongs to,
    running the Windows binary under wine when it is there. Both of the
    cross-platform faults above compiled cleanly and would have shipped.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 08 Sep 2026 10:20:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.9.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * THE FIRST SCREEN SAYS WHICH VERSION IT IS. The menu is the way in for
    most people and it was the one screen that never named the build, so
    somebody reporting that something does not work was looking at it with
    no way to answer "which version". The only route was to quit and run
    `hub --version`, which means leaving the part of it they know how to
    use. It sits on the title line rather than a line of its own, because
    rows there are a fixed budget shared with the content.
  * A test guards it. Until now nothing could assert on a screen at all:
    Frame builds the display into a private field and only the code that
    paints the terminal ever read it, which is part of why this went
    unnoticed through nine releases. Frame gains a test-only way to read
    back what was drawn, and the guard was checked by removing the version
    again and confirming it fails.
  * Costs nothing: 992,592 bytes, the same as 0.9.2 to the byte. The
    version string was already in the binary for `--version`.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Sun, 07 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.9.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * THE CABLE ADDRESS IS VISIBLE AGAIN. Sending down a cable said "no cable
    address yet. Wait half a minute and look again." forever, while holding
    a perfectly good address and serving on it. The probe that finds a cable
    aims at 169.254.255.255, which is a broadcast address, and connecting a
    socket to one without asking for broadcast permission is refused by the
    kernel every time. So the probe written to fix this exact symptom had
    never once succeeded. One line. It also restores two things nobody knew
    were missing: `hub doctor` now lists the cable address alongside the
    wifi one, and the sending screen offers `gorilla.local` again, which was
    only ever printed when a cable address was known.
  * Port 80 says what is actually wrong. It reported "port 80 belongs to
    something else" whatever the cause, and on Linux the usual cause is not
    a clash at all: ports under 1024 need a permission, so the bind is
    refused while port 80 sits empty. Being told a free port is taken sends
    people hunting a web server that does not exist. It now separates the
    two, and when it is a permission it names sudo and the .deb.
  * The Arch package declared the wrong licence. packaging/PKGBUILD said
    MIT while the LICENSE file, the Debian copyright and both README.txt
    files said AGPL-3.0, so pacman reported one licence and the package
    installed the text of another. The value was written out in two files,
    which is how they drifted, so build-arch.sh now reads it from the
    PKGBUILD and a test fails if either stops naming the AGPL.
  * The test suite stopped failing at random. Running it gave one failure
    per run and a different test each time, while running it one test at a
    time passed every time. The device-blocking tests all talk to 127.0.0.1
    and cargo runs them in parallel, so one test blocked the address another
    was mid-request on. They now take a lock. A suite that fails randomly
    teaches people to re-run it until it is green.
  * Installing tells you when port 80 was not granted, instead of saying
    nothing. The capability is a Recommends, which apt honours and `dpkg -i`
    does not, and `dpkg -i` is what the release page tells people to run.
  * This changelog's top entry had been stamped with each new version while
    still describing the 0.8.0 folder work, so recent packages shipped the
    wrong release notes under the right number. Frozen at 0.8.0 where it
    belongs. 0.9.0 and 0.9.1 have no entry here and none is invented; their
    notes are on the GitHub releases page.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Sun, 07 Sep 2026 17:00:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * FOLDERS ARE HANDED OUT. Pointing this at a folder used to offer only
    the files sitting loose at the top level, while everything inside a
    subfolder was silently left out of every list. A teacher's folder has
    folders in it: subjects, weeks, a resource pack as downloaded. On a
    real 68,153-file folder the old behaviour offered six files.
  * Get a whole folder in one go. The receiving screen could fetch exactly
    one selected file, so a pack of 200 files meant 200 selections. Press
    `a` and it takes all of them, rebuilding the folder structure.
  * Fixed a matching hole: a file inside a subfolder was never listed but
    was still served to anybody who guessed its path. The permission check
    looked at the first part of the path instead of the whole of it.
  * A file one folder down can be READ in the browser now, not only
    downloaded.
  * The class page shows the first 300 files and says how many more there
    are, rather than sending a phone a page it cannot lay out.
  * The wifi channel can be chosen when making the network, on the screen
    and as --channel. The list of channels offered is read from the radio
    and the country, not hardcoded: a laptop in another regulatory domain
    offers its own lanes, including 5 GHz on hardware that has it. Found
    the hard way: the automatic choice picked channel 1 and a download
    that ran at 7 MB/s the day before ran at 4.7, with the radio holding
    87% of the airtime to do it.
  * GET EVERYTHING: one purple button on the page hands the whole folder
    over as a single uncompressed zip, streamed as it is built, with an
    exact size so the browser shows a progress bar with an end. Windows
    opens it like a folder with nothing installed. Store mode on purpose:
    the 2026-08-22 ruling against compressing on a weak laptop stands;
    this is boxing, not squeezing.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 19:00:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.7.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A laptop is now identified in the permanent record. A device is asked
    its name when it joins the network, and phones answer; laptops very
    often answer nothing, so a note from one was filed against a bare
    address. The device column is the one a child cannot argue with, so it
    now falls back to what the browser says: "a Windows laptop", "a Linux
    laptop", "an iPhone". Only that description is kept, never the header
    it came from.
  * Who is on the network, and what each device is called, is worked out on
    a tick whatever screen is showing. It used to happen only while the
    roster was being drawn, so a device that joined while the teacher was
    reading waiting work was never looked up at all.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 13:20:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Two ways to take a device off, and the screen says which is which.
    Pause one from the roster (c, then space): it keeps the wifi and loses
    the lesson, seeing a page that says its teacher paused it and nothing
    else. That page refreshes itself, so letting them back in needs nothing
    from the child. A pause is keyed to the device, not the address, so
    forgetting the network and rejoining does not shake it off.
  * Change the wifi password and restart the network (c, then p): the
    heavier control, for a device that comes back wearing a different name.
    Pauses deliberately survive it.
  * A paused device that leaves the room keeps its row, because otherwise
    there is no way left to un-pause it.
  * Join by camera (j): a QR code drawn without a dependency. The network
    name and password stay printed underneath at the same size; plenty of
    these phones cannot scan, and a screen showing only a code locks those
    children out invisibly.
  * Fixed: restoring the teacher's own wifi failed silently for any network
    with a colon in its name, because the name was fed back still escaped.
    That is the exact failure the restore path exists to prevent.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 12:56:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Work a child sends now WAITS for the teacher. It lands in a holding
    area, the roster says "N pieces of work waiting for you", and she
    accepts or refuses each one (w on the roster; a, r, o to look). Nothing
    reaches her folder unaccepted and nothing sent to her is ever handed
    back out to the class, not even while it waits. A refusal is moved to
    refused/, never deleted: it may be evidence.
  * Several files in one send. One assignment is rarely one file, and the
    parser stopped at the first. It walks every part now.
  * Office and school formats declared properly: doc, docx, xls, xlsx,
    ppt, pptx, odt, ods, odp, rtf, epub, zip, 7z, csv, md, xml, json, svg.
  * The transfer row says which way the bytes are going and how long is
    left, so a teacher knows whether she can shut the lid. A finished row
    no longer reads "99% done".

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 11:00:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.5.1) unstable; urgency=high

  * A serve bound to one address no longer also grabs port 80. It used to
    take port 80 on whatever host was asked for, so anything bound to
    127.0.0.1 blocked a real 0.0.0.0:80 with EADDRINUSE and silently cost
    the hotspot its sign-in screen. Only a wildcard serve claims it now.
  * A device's chosen name is filed under the device, not its address.
    Addresses are recycled: the next child to join could be handed the
    address a previous one had and silently inherit their name, which
    corrupts the attribution the name exists for. A reconnecting phone
    also keeps its name instead of being asked again.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 10:45:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Notes now carry a timestamp and a stable device tag:
    "26-08-25 10:35  Amina #wa3x [Xiaomi-11-Lite-5G-NE, 10.42.0.90]: ...".
    Thirty children typing the same name are now thirty distinguishable
    lines. The tag is four characters hashed from the hardware address, so
    it survives a reconnect (a real test produced two notes from ONE phone
    under two different addresses) while putting no permanent identifier
    for somebody else's device on a screen or in a repo.
  * The roster names any name that two devices are both claiming, and
    shows the tag beside those entries only. The teacher is told, rather
    than left to work it out.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 10:38:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.4.2) unstable; urgency=high

  * Fix worker starvation that made the server stop answering while looking
    healthy. The class page refreshed its list every 6 seconds while an idle
    kept-alive connection held a worker for 30, so every device parked about
    five workers permanently: thirty children would need 150 and the pool
    has 64. Idle connections now release after 5 seconds, a real transfer
    still gets the full 30. Measured with 16 workers and 90 idle
    connections: before, a latecomer timed out after 8 seconds; after, it
    was served immediately.
  * The escape hatch is a BUTTON, not an address to type. Mobile browsers
    autocomplete from history and rewrite what was typed, so printing
    "go to 10.42.0.1" sent a real user to a cached 10.42.0.1:8080 that was
    not answering. A link is never autofilled. Three layers: an Android
    intent:// breakout, a plain link, and the menu instruction.
  * The file list refreshes every 10 seconds instead of 6.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 10:20:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The roster shows the picked name AND the device: "Johnny [Xiaomi-11-
    Lite-5G-NE]". The name was typed by a kid and can be denied; the device
    model was reported by the phone itself and cannot.
  * A loud escape hatch under hand-in. The wifi sign-in window is a
    stripped WebView with no file chooser wired up, so "Choose file" does
    nothing and the page looks broken. It now says so and prints the
