gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=high

  * Fix: the wifi-restore safety fuse fired INTO the running lesson every
    three to four minutes, because a systemd timer's deadline cannot be
    pushed back by re-arming under the same name. The fuse is now one
    transient service holding a sleep, and the heartbeat restarts it, which
    truly moves the deadline. Proven with a short fuse before shipping:
    beats push it back, abandonment fires it, stopping disarms it.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 08:35:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The class page: a phone that joins the wifi gets its own sign-in screen
    popped by the operating system, and the sign-in screen is the lesson.
    Big buttons, works with no JavaScript on browsers back to 2009.
  * Hand-in: kids send work back from the same page. Retries land once,
    a changed resubmission is kept as a second version, and nothing in
    handed-in/ is ever served back to the class.
  * Notes: a kid can send a short note to the teacher; the teacher can put
    a notice at the top of every kid's page.
  * The tick list: the teacher chooses file by file what the network can
    see, before starting and live during the lesson.
  * Serves on port 80 as well as 8080 (the install grants that one
    capability), and answers the connectivity probes of Android, iOS,
    Windows, GNOME, KDE and Firefox via a NetworkManager dnsmasq drop-in.
  * USB drives: hand-in switches itself off loudly on a read-only drive,
    uploads are synced before they get their final name, and the roster
    says so if the folder vanishes mid-lesson.

 -- gorillanobakaa <gorillanobakaa@gmail.com>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 08:20:00 +0100

gorilla-portable-network-hub (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First package. One binary with a screen and four commands: serve, get,
    sums and doctor.
  * Running "hub" with no arguments opens the screen rather than printing
    usage, which is what a double-click passes.
  * Creates a WPA2 wifi network through NetworkManager, never an open one,
    and restores the previous connection through a systemd timer that
    survives the program being killed.
  * Finds the machine handing out files by asking the gateway first, then
    sweeping the local /24.
  * Byte ranges and keep-alive on both sides, so an interrupted transfer
    carries on instead of starting again.

